  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 AM EST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\n- Dry and mild through Friday\n\nIR loop shows clear skies over the region with some cirrus over MN\nflowing in this direction. A dome of high pressure aloft and at\nthe surface is responsible for the clear skies. We'll see another\nsunny day with above normal temperatures. As the upper ridge axis\nmoves toward Lower MI, heights will rise a bit more and surface\ntemperatures will continue a slow climb into mid 50s Wednesday and\n60 by Thursday and Friday. \n\n- Weekend showers, turning colder\n\nSaturday will be the warmest day of the week as we climb into the\nlower to mid 60s across the cwa. A short wave moving east across\nthe Rockies will create a surface low in MN which will then track\nnortheast toward James Bay and drag a cold front toward the cwa\nSaturday. Strong moisture convergence ahead of the front will \nhelp to generate light rain showers Saturday afternoon and\nespecially Saturday night and then diminishing Sunday. Instability\nahead of the front is very low and we don't anticipate thunder\nwith the showers at this time. Once the cold front moves through,\nh8 temps will fall from 8c Sunday morning to -2c by Sunday night.\nHigh temperatures Monday and Tuesday will only be in the lower\n40s.",
